1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:02,790
Hi students. In this video we're

2
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:04,620
going to talk about
Linux filesystems.

3
00:00:04,620 --> 00:00:07,710
We're going to talk about
understanding the use of files,

4
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:09,345
how files are used in Linux.

5
00:00:09,345 --> 00:00:11,820
We're going to introduce
inodes and Links as

6
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:14,955
two useful parts of
Linux filesystems.

7
00:00:14,955 --> 00:00:16,980
We'll just talk about how Linux

8
00:00:16,980 --> 00:00:19,540
filesystem work in general.

9
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:22,065
Linux uses what's called the

10
00:00:22,065 --> 00:00:23,895
"Everything Is A File" paradigm,

11
00:00:23,895 --> 00:00:25,740
meaning a lot of the interaction

12
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:26,865
that you do with the kernel,

13
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:28,845
whether it's what
you think of as

14
00:00:28,845 --> 00:00:31,530
a typical file
holding, for instance,

15
00:00:31,530 --> 00:00:33,330
user data or if you're

16
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:35,400
interacting with the
hardware device in

17
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:36,565
a lot of the case that

18
00:00:36,565 --> 00:00:39,740
happens via reading
and writing of files.

19
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:41,660
For instance, if you're
interacting with

20
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:43,715
the USB serial port,

21
00:00:43,715 --> 00:00:46,500
you're probably reading and
writing to dev/ttyUSB0.

22
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:50,120
The benefit of this
is if you've got

23
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:51,605
a generic piece of

24
00:00:51,605 --> 00:00:54,320
software that can
open, read, and write,

25
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:57,530
you can probably redirect
that software to either

26
00:00:57,530 --> 00:00:58,670
what you'd think of as

27
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:00,800
a typical file
containing user data,

28
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,660
or you could
potentially redirect it

29
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:04,580
to something like
USB serial port

30
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:06,320
and have it operate on

31
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:08,805
stream of data coming
over the serial port.

32
00:01:08,805 --> 00:01:13,310
This makes it much more
versatile and more easy

33
00:01:13,310 --> 00:01:15,380
to write software
that can work in

34
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:18,860
different environments
or different scenarios.

35
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:22,405
Files in Linux are
represented by paths.

36
00:01:22,405 --> 00:01:24,155
There's two different
ways that you can

37
00:01:24,155 --> 00:01:25,850
access a file based on path.

38
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:28,520
One is absolute, for instance,

39
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:32,120
if you had, /path/to/file,

40
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:36,260
each one of these slashes
would separate a directory and

41
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:38,060
the starting slash would
indicate that you're

42
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:40,429
starting from root of
the file hierarchy.

43
00:01:40,429 --> 00:01:44,390
Whereas if you use path/to/file
with no proceeding slash,

44
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:45,950
what this would
mean is that from

45
00:01:45,950 --> 00:01:47,795
whatever working
directory you're in,

46
00:01:47,795 --> 00:01:49,550
you would move relative to

47
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:51,620
that current directory
so the leading slash

48
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:53,105
tells you whether it's

49
00:01:53,105 --> 00:01:56,700
an absolute or relative path
that you're referencing.

50
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:59,720
For a regular file, what
we normally think of

51
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:02,315
as a file or a file containing,

52
00:02:02,315 --> 00:02:04,855
for instance, user
content, really,

53
00:02:04,855 --> 00:02:06,940
this is just bytes of
data in a byte stream

54
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:09,215
and the system doesn't
have any expectation

55
00:02:09,215 --> 00:02:11,060
about what's actually contained

56
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:14,435
in what's called a regular file.

57
00:02:14,435 --> 00:02:16,535
The file position and

58
00:02:16,535 --> 00:02:19,310
offset contract the location
so when you're reading,

59
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:20,990
if you call the
next read command,

60
00:02:20,990 --> 00:02:22,610
the kernel will keep track

61
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:24,440
for you about where you're at in

62
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:26,180
the file and will
automatically give

63
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:28,600
you the next chunk of
data that you read.

64
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:30,870
Files can also be truncated

65
00:02:30,870 --> 00:02:32,570
and they can be
truncated to a size,

66
00:02:32,570 --> 00:02:34,940
smaller so you can
cut down the content

67
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:37,430
to a specific length or
to zero, for instance,

68
00:02:37,430 --> 00:02:39,110
if you want to start with

69
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:41,370
a completely new content

70
00:02:41,370 --> 00:02:43,130
or you can also truncate

71
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:44,660
the file larger
than the original,

72
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:46,495
which sounds a little
counter-intuitive.

73
00:02:46,495 --> 00:02:49,100
But what you can do
is define a file of

74
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:52,550
a specific size without
reserving that space on disk.

75
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:55,070
If there's a reason
for you to have

76
00:02:55,070 --> 00:02:57,980
a file that's defined
with a specific size,

77
00:02:57,980 --> 00:02:59,495
but maybe you don't
need to write

78
00:02:59,495 --> 00:03:02,075
the entire file full
of content initially,

79
00:03:02,075 --> 00:03:03,500
you can actually use

80
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:07,700
file system capabilities
to define that file of

81
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:10,940
a specific size but not use
up the disk space initially

82
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:15,300
at least until it's been
written by another application.

83
00:03:15,350 --> 00:03:18,105
Files can be opened
more than once,

84
00:03:18,105 --> 00:03:19,685
they can be opened by

85
00:03:19,685 --> 00:03:23,875
different processes or the
same process at the same time.

86
00:03:23,875 --> 00:03:26,810
File is ultimately
referenced and accessed

87
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:29,915
by what's called an inode
in the file system.

88
00:03:29,915 --> 00:03:34,370
Linux inode stands for Index
Node and this is really

89
00:03:34,370 --> 00:03:36,290
just a piece of
metadata that's used to

90
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:39,140
track where files
are located on disk.

91
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:40,940
You can think of
it as a database

92
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:42,275
that the file system's
going to keep

93
00:03:42,275 --> 00:03:45,430
about all the different files
and their location on disk.

94
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:47,300
In addition to the
location on disk,

95
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:50,090
the inode is going to store
things like the timestamp,

96
00:03:50,090 --> 00:03:51,290
the owner of the file,

97
00:03:51,290 --> 00:03:52,505
the size of the file,

98
00:03:52,505 --> 00:03:54,360
and the mode or the
access permissions

99
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:56,620
so who should be allowed
to access the file.

100
00:03:56,620 --> 00:04:01,350
This is a small size,
a chunk of data.

101
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:03,230
you think of something
like a 128 bytes

102
00:04:03,230 --> 00:04:05,060
in size and one thing to note

103
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:06,830
about this is that the filename

104
00:04:06,830 --> 00:04:08,850
is not included in the inode,

105
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:10,550
that might be a little
counter-intuitive when you think

106
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:13,085
about locating files
in the file system.

107
00:04:13,085 --> 00:04:16,430
How do we map a
filename to an inode?

108
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:18,620
That's really the
function of a directory.

109
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:21,410
The directory is really
just a set of files with

110
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:23,450
their own inodes that contains

111
00:04:23,450 --> 00:04:25,955
a mapping of filename
to an inode.

112
00:04:25,955 --> 00:04:27,650
A directory entry is simply

113
00:04:27,650 --> 00:04:30,410
a name that corresponds
to the file that you're

114
00:04:30,410 --> 00:04:32,960
looking up and an inode
reference the points

115
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:36,215
to a specific inode in
the directory table.

116
00:04:36,215 --> 00:04:38,600
The directory start again at

117
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:40,775
the root directory
so slash would be

118
00:04:40,775 --> 00:04:41,930
the root directory where all

119
00:04:41,930 --> 00:04:43,430
directories start and then you

120
00:04:43,430 --> 00:04:44,810
can have a sub hierarchy of

121
00:04:44,810 --> 00:04:46,780
directories created from there.

122
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:51,240
Each one of the directory
entries would have

123
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:52,550
an inode that points to

124
00:04:52,550 --> 00:04:56,830
the corresponding new
referenced file or directory.

125
00:04:56,830 --> 00:05:00,890
You might ask, why wouldn't
we have the filename

126
00:05:00,890 --> 00:05:02,030
built into the I node

127
00:05:02,030 --> 00:05:04,980
if this is associated
with the file?

128
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:08,555
The reason is the design
to keep the filename

129
00:05:08,555 --> 00:05:10,070
out of the inode allows us to

130
00:05:10,070 --> 00:05:11,840
reference the file
in multiple ways,

131
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:15,065
we can reference the same file
content in multiple ways.

132
00:05:15,065 --> 00:05:17,150
The things that are
associated with

133
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:19,755
a file like the size, the UID,

134
00:05:19,755 --> 00:05:21,755
user and group ID,

135
00:05:21,755 --> 00:05:24,005
the permission location on disk,

136
00:05:24,005 --> 00:05:25,370
all that needs to be constant

137
00:05:25,370 --> 00:05:27,755
regardless of how
we access the file.

138
00:05:27,755 --> 00:05:29,840
However, it might
make sense to access

139
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:33,230
the file from two
different places

140
00:05:33,230 --> 00:05:34,670
and get the same content.

141
00:05:34,670 --> 00:05:37,370
That's really the function
of a link and so the inode

142
00:05:37,370 --> 00:05:39,890
designed by keeping the
filename out of the inode,

143
00:05:39,890 --> 00:05:41,630
we have the option
to access it from

144
00:05:41,630 --> 00:05:44,215
different places and
get the same content.

145
00:05:44,215 --> 00:05:47,390
The link flexibility
means that we can

146
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:50,090
have multiple names that
resolved to the same inode

147
00:05:50,090 --> 00:05:51,830
and the same file content

148
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:55,310
permissions that are
associated with that inode.

149
00:05:55,310 --> 00:05:57,890
Links can redirect
two different file

150
00:05:57,890 --> 00:05:59,930
and directory paths
to the same inode.

151
00:05:59,930 --> 00:06:01,700
Here's an example of if you do

152
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:04,895
an ls-l on a directory
that contains a link,

153
00:06:04,895 --> 00:06:09,840
you'll see a symbol like this
indicating that my link is

154
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:15,140
a link to my file.txt
so if I open my link,

155
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:16,670
I will get the content of my

156
00:06:16,670 --> 00:06:21,125
file.txt the same as I would
if I just open my file.txt.

157
00:06:21,125 --> 00:06:23,390
There's two types of Links.

158
00:06:23,390 --> 00:06:26,825
In Linux, the hard and
the symbolic link,

159
00:06:26,825 --> 00:06:29,780
the symbolic link is often
referred to as a symlink.

160
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:32,570
Hard links map
directly to inodes,

161
00:06:32,570 --> 00:06:33,830
and for that reason,
they are only

162
00:06:33,830 --> 00:06:35,330
allowed on the same file system.

163
00:06:35,330 --> 00:06:37,280
The inode is referencing

164
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:39,380
metadata that's talking
about the currents,

165
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:41,585
the structure of one
specific file system.

166
00:06:41,585 --> 00:06:43,055
For instance, it has

167
00:06:43,055 --> 00:06:44,750
a specific location on disk

168
00:06:44,750 --> 00:06:46,655
that's tied to that file system.

169
00:06:46,655 --> 00:06:48,770
For that reason, the hard
link is only going to

170
00:06:48,770 --> 00:06:51,080
work across the
same file system.

171
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:55,475
But a soft link or a symlink
just maps to a file name.

172
00:06:55,475 --> 00:06:58,820
That means that they could
work across any file system.

173
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:01,430
Really, anything in the
directory hierarchy

174
00:07:01,430 --> 00:07:03,870
can be referenced
by a soft link.

175
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:07,970
Again, if you talk
about a hard link,

176
00:07:07,970 --> 00:07:11,150
it's really conceptually the
same as a directory entry.

177
00:07:11,150 --> 00:07:13,700
It works and acts the same way.

178
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:16,940
Because we're using
the same file system,

179
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:19,670
we know when it's safe
to delete the file based

180
00:07:19,670 --> 00:07:22,970
on whether or not there are
references to the file.

181
00:07:22,970 --> 00:07:25,745
If the hard link was
created initially

182
00:07:25,745 --> 00:07:29,945
and we delete the
original directory entry,

183
00:07:29,945 --> 00:07:32,480
we can know that we still
can't allow delete of the file

184
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:33,710
because there's
still a reference

185
00:07:33,710 --> 00:07:36,140
to it from the hard link.

186
00:07:36,140 --> 00:07:39,515
Symbolic links or symlinks
on the other hand,

187
00:07:39,515 --> 00:07:43,850
those aren't going to be
directly referencing an inode,

188
00:07:43,850 --> 00:07:45,125
instead, they're going
to be referencing

189
00:07:45,125 --> 00:07:47,210
a path in the file system.

190
00:07:47,210 --> 00:07:48,560
They'll look
something like this.

191
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:50,570
They'll have their
own inode pointing to

192
00:07:50,570 --> 00:07:52,580
a location on disk
and that location

193
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:53,960
on disk is simply going to have

194
00:07:53,960 --> 00:07:56,930
a path that references
the file system.

195
00:07:56,930 --> 00:07:58,370
That path is going to reference

196
00:07:58,370 --> 00:08:01,040
a specific directory
entry and we can use

197
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:02,090
that inode to find

198
00:08:02,090 --> 00:08:05,765
the content in the file
that we want to access.

199
00:08:05,765 --> 00:08:07,850
You can see that this
is going to take

200
00:08:07,850 --> 00:08:10,220
one extra step to
resolve for us because

201
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:11,960
we have to look up for

202
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:14,240
the directory entry that's
corresponding to the symlink.

203
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:16,250
We have to look at
the symbolic link,

204
00:08:16,250 --> 00:08:17,630
find the location on disk,

205
00:08:17,630 --> 00:08:19,775
read the symbolic
link dislocation,

206
00:08:19,775 --> 00:08:22,250
and link that into the
directory entry to ultimately

207
00:08:22,250 --> 00:08:25,620
find the inode for the file
content that we care about.

208
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:28,760
We're talking, so far,

209
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:30,335
about regular files,

210
00:08:30,335 --> 00:08:33,380
but there's also the
concept in Linux of

211
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:35,060
special files and I mentioned

212
00:08:35,060 --> 00:08:38,180
the dev/ttyUSB0 as
an example of this.

213
00:08:38,180 --> 00:08:40,205
What you can do with
a special file is

214
00:08:40,205 --> 00:08:43,310
map hardware devices to

215
00:08:43,310 --> 00:08:46,020
the "everything is
a file" paradigm.

216
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:50,440
Special files are
really kernel objects

217
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:52,090
that are represented as files.

218
00:08:52,090 --> 00:08:55,330
Those could be character
devices or block devices,

219
00:08:55,330 --> 00:08:58,605
they also could be
named pipes or sockets.

220
00:08:58,605 --> 00:09:03,470
Character device is a device
that's used anytime you

221
00:09:03,470 --> 00:09:05,510
have a linear queue of bytes in

222
00:09:05,510 --> 00:09:07,820
terms of the interaction
with the device.

223
00:09:07,820 --> 00:09:09,935
A great example of
that is a keyboard.

224
00:09:09,935 --> 00:09:11,825
If you're pressing a
key on a keyboard,

225
00:09:11,825 --> 00:09:14,060
there's a sequence of bytes
that you're sending that's

226
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:17,030
associated with the entries
as you hit the key,

227
00:09:17,030 --> 00:09:20,135
so that would be a great
example of a character device.

228
00:09:20,135 --> 00:09:22,025
A block device, on
the other hand,

229
00:09:22,025 --> 00:09:25,880
would be when you're
accessing data in bulk and so

230
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:30,050
you access a entire chunk
of data at one time.

231
00:09:30,050 --> 00:09:31,445
An example of this would be

232
00:09:31,445 --> 00:09:34,565
a hard disk drive where
you're going to access on

233
00:09:34,565 --> 00:09:36,680
file system
boundaries and access

234
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:39,785
more than one byte at
a time, typically.

235
00:09:39,785 --> 00:09:42,290
Then the named pipes and sockets

236
00:09:42,290 --> 00:09:44,044
are used for inter-process
communication,

237
00:09:44,044 --> 00:09:47,940
which we'll get to in later
videos for this week.

238
00:09:48,310 --> 00:09:51,170
The file system is
ultimately a collection of

239
00:09:51,170 --> 00:09:53,480
files in a hierarchy and there's

240
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:54,785
lots of different
file systems to

241
00:09:54,785 --> 00:09:56,720
choose from that
are ultimately tied

242
00:09:56,720 --> 00:10:00,275
to how the file content
is stored on disk.

243
00:10:00,275 --> 00:10:01,940
NFS is an example of

244
00:10:01,940 --> 00:10:04,430
a file system that's used
for network file storage.

245
00:10:04,430 --> 00:10:07,940
Ext 4 is a common
block device storage.

246
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:10,940
There's a whole series
of ext file systems

247
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:12,440
and those are probably
the most common that are

248
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:14,225
used on Linux devices.

249
00:10:14,225 --> 00:10:15,680
There's also fat, which is

250
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:18,080
a Microsoft defined
storage format

251
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:22,639
for disks that can inter-operate
with Windows platforms.

252
00:10:22,639 --> 00:10:25,775
The Linux file system is
mounted and unmounted

253
00:10:25,775 --> 00:10:28,775
to add and remove to
the root file system.

254
00:10:28,775 --> 00:10:30,830
The paths that we talked about,

255
00:10:30,830 --> 00:10:33,485
the absolute paths, you
can create a mount point,

256
00:10:33,485 --> 00:10:37,145
you can mount a new file
system to that mount point,

257
00:10:37,145 --> 00:10:38,330
and then you can access it

258
00:10:38,330 --> 00:10:39,830
through absolute
paths just like you

259
